    historyContent: {
      origins: "Kaluki emerged in the mid-20th century as an evolution of standard rummy games, likely developing simultaneously in British and North American card clubs. The name 'Kalooki' may derive from Eastern European Jewish communities who popularized the game in London and New York.",
      originCountries: [
        'United Kingdom',
        'USA',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1940s: Two-deck rummy variants gain popularity in US and Europe',
        "1950s: 'Kalooki 51' becomes standardized in UK bridge and card clubs",
        '1960s: British and North American rules diverge slightly in melding requirements',
        '1990s: Online gaming platforms popularize Kaluki globally',
        'Present: A staple in European and North American card clubs, especially popular in Jewish communities',
      ],
      evolution: 'The game developed from standard rummy by adding the initial meld threshold (40 or 51 points) and Joker exchange mechanics. British versions typically use 40-point openings while North American versions favor 51 points.',
      cultural: "Kaluki is particularly associated with Jewish communities in London and New York, where it became a social institution in card clubs. The game is known for being 'polite' but intensely competitive, often played alongside bridge.",
    },
